{"loc":"ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-5-english:p335","rows":[{"verses":["24:38"],"words":["*"],"type":"grammar","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"The lām in verse 24:38 expresses consequence, not cause; Qur'an 28:8 and an Imruʾ al-Qays couplet support this usage, with the couplet's “on” carrying the consequential sense.","anchor":"Here too the on is meant to express the consequence.","mentions":["28:8"]},{"verses":["24:38"],"words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Their deeds, done from fear of the Hereafter and desire for God's pleasure, bring reward exceeding the deeds and further grace; they are forgiven beyond their imagining.","anchor":"for fear of the Hereafter and to earn the pleasure of God will be a source of a reward that surpasses their deeds","mentions":[]},{"verses":["24:39"],"words":["*"],"type":"links","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"After the believer parable, the disbeliever parable depicts their inner and outer selves; its darkness is equal to the believers' light.","anchor":"After mentioning the parable of belief and its bearers, now a parable of disbelief","mentions":[]},{"verses":["24:39"],"words":["*"],"type":"rhetoric","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"The believers' parable presents faith first as cause and deeds as effect; the disbelievers' parable reverses that order, showing their deeds first.","anchor":"In the parable of the believers, first the effect-producer (faith) is highlighted and then the effectee (deed).","mentions":[]},{"verses":["24:39"],"words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Faith is rooted deeply in human nature, with its blessed results visible in conduct; disbelief sprouts from filth, and its poisonous fruits are visible deeds without roots in human nature.","anchor":"faith is a very deep reality whose roots have penetrated deep into the nature of a human being","mentions":[]},{"verses":["24:39"],"words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Disbelievers will find their deeds vanished like a mirage; instead they meet their Lord, who settles their account in the blink of an eye.","anchor":"they will encounter their Lord and He will settle their account in the blink of an eye.","mentions":[]},{"verses":["24:39"],"words":["*"],"type":"interpretation","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"The words through the verse's end picture the disbelievers' misfortune and deprivation in the Hereafter, completing the sense as ‘until when it comes …’.","anchor":"Since the purpose of the parable is to explain their deprivation in the Hereafter","mentions":[]}]}
